1. What is being insured, and what the control does
===================================================

The proposal concerns agents capable of consequential actions: transactions, data disclosures, tool calls, infrastructure changes and delegated work. It does not claim to establish model correctness, eliminate fraud, supply a legal liability shield or guarantee lower premiums.

Principal means the person or organisation granting authority. Agent means the actor exercising it. Relying party means the person or system accepting or acting upon the agent’s request, such as a merchant, API operator or counterparty; it need not be the insured. Mandate means a signed, scoped delegation binding the principal, agent, permitted actions/resources, validity and lifecycle conditions.

Authority contraction is used descriptively for non-expanding delegated or task-specific authority. It is aligned with the narrowing and monotonicity concepts in the reviewed GAuth/CCPE materials, not introduced as a new protocol primitive or product name.

Evidence labels used throughout:

- Documented: supported by the identified source, not independently certified.

- Proposed: a collaboration or pilot requirement, not a deployed interface.

- Hypothesis: a benefit to measure, not a proven result.

- Unknown: not established by reviewed evidence; not proof that a capability cannot exist.

The GAuth name is used for the open-standard/open-core authority approach, subject to applicable designation rules. It does not rename every commercial Gimel service “GAuth”. No rights to implementations, patents, marks or proprietary combinations are granted by this memo.

Reading guide for insurance teams
---------------------------------

In this document, a mandate is a machine-verifiable record of delegated business authority, not an insurance mandate, policy endorsement or automatic legal power of attorney. Runtime enforcement means checking and applying that authority immediately before an action is executed. Monotonicity, also described as contraction, means that a delegated assignment can become narrower but cannot silently become broader. A platform decision is the destination system’s own permission check. S1 and S2 are technical architecture labels, not insurance ratings; their underwriting significance is explained in §8.4. A control boundary is the set of transaction routes actually protected, not the whole enterprise unless that scope has been demonstrated.

