4.3 Relying-party knowledge
---------------------------

“The relying party knew” should not be a single automatically inferred field. Distinguish information that was:

- Available for retrieval;

- Delivered to an identified endpoint;

- Acknowledged by an identified system or person;

- Evaluated with a recorded verification result;

- Overridden or ignored before execution.

A signed acknowledgement proves only what its authenticated semantics and supporting evidence establish. It is not conclusive proof of human understanding or legally attributable organisational knowledge.

Actual or constructive knowledge, apparent authority, ratification, contractual verification duties, causation and mandatory law require jurisdiction-specific review. Relying-party knowledge can be material without extinguishing another participant’s liability. Evidence supporting recovery or contribution should not prematurely assign fault.

