Integrity protection, issuer authentication and action binding should make alteration and substitution detectable. Signatures do not establish completeness or truthfulness when issuers are compromised or paths are uninstrumented.

Agree versioned semantics, reproducible verification, omission/replay/clock-skew tests, incident preservation and documented custody. Missing evidence means unknown, not automatic compliance, fault or claim denial.

Minimise personal and proprietary data. The insurer should receive necessary assessment/claims evidence rather than unrestricted raw prompts, biometric material, confidential business records or credentials. Use access controls, lawful processing, redaction, retention/deletion schedules and appropriate cross-border arrangements. Hashes and pseudonyms are not automatically anonymous. Privilege and lawful evidence preservation need separate review.

7. What an underwriter can test and potentially recognise
=========================================================

7.1 Potential benefits and counter-effects
------------------------------------------

Verified controls may reduce unauthorised-action frequency, constrain loss severity, limit repeated/aggregate exposure and improve investigation. Better evidence may reduce uncertainty or claims-handling cost. Conversely, it may expose previously hidden risk and increase assessed exposure. False denials, latency and control outages may generate business interruption costs.

Insurers retain their own valuation methods. Conformance is evidence about a defined control scope, not an actuarial rating.

7.2 Exposure is not a premium formula
-------------------------------------

Economic value at risk depends on downstream effects, recovery, repetition and dependence. A EUR 10,000 transaction cap does not cap all resulting damage at EUR 10,000.

If statistical Value at Risk is used, specify the confidence level, time horizon and loss distribution. Consider expected loss and tail measures such as expected shortfall as well. A short pilot cannot validate rare extreme losses.

Premiums also reflect expenses, capital, reinsurance, limits, deductibles, exclusions and uncertainty. No discount or premium formula is proposed. A carrier may respond through different limits, deductibles, evidence terms, exclusions or no change.

7.3 Measurement plan
--------------------

Use exposure-normalised measures:

- attempted violations, actual losses and action volume;

- execution after denial, bypass rate and repeated-action exposure;

- revocation propagation and end-to-end enforcement latency;

- evidence completeness and successful incident reconstruction;

- severity, recovery and aggregate/correlated loss where observable;

- false denials, overhead and business interruption;

- changes in users, models, action mix, connectors and operating conditions.

Compare matched workloads, historical baselines or safe simulations; document confounders, sample size, uncertainty and selection effects. Blocked attempts are not automatically avoided claims. Red-team tests establish control behaviour, not actuarial loss reduction. Do not remove essential safeguards from live high-risk operations to construct a control group.

8. aiSure and Instnt illustrations
==================================

8.1 Munich Re aiSure
--------------------

Documented: Munich Re describes aiSure as covering specified AI-related contractual liabilities, own financial losses and legal liabilities. Its FAQ discusses development-pipeline review, data drift, statistical risk estimation and performance-risk pricing research. Its corporate factsheet describes tailored cover, deductibles, co-insurance, sub-limits and insurable penalties.[1–3]

A proposed collaboration would select one insured agent use case, separate performance errors from authority failures, validate GAuth controls and compare resulting exposure/evidence. The insurer would decide whether the findings affect terms.

An unauthorised action is not automatically an insured “AI error”. Depending on cause and wording, it may intersect with cyber, crime, professional indemnity, technology errors and omissions, contractual liability or other cover—or be excluded. A performance warranty needs its own measurable insured event; authority conformance alone proves neither accuracy nor ROI.

No reviewed source establishes Munich Re acceptance of GAuth, an integration, a discount or a claims format.

8.2 Instnt: identity/fraud decisioning, not merely a content guardrail
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Documented: Munich Re’s 2026 case study describes Instnt’s Identity Decision Model, using behavioural, device, document-authenticity, digital-footprint and identity-bureau signals to estimate fraud risk and expected loss, combined with fraud-loss insurance backed by a reinsurance panel led by Munich Re.[4]

This is substantive risk decisioning plus financial protection. It should not be reduced to an LLM content filter. Marketing language about complete liability transfer or zero fraud losses is not repeated here as an unconditional promise; policy terms and availability govern.

Evidence-bounded classification: on the delegated-authority axis, the described identity-based arrangement is S1-aligned: the case study does not demonstrate a separate principal-signed, scoped, revocable agent mandate and action-bound enforcement. This is an analogy for the described baseline in an agent deployment, not certification of Instnt, a complete architecture audit, or proof that all private implementations lack these capabilities. Ordinary identity-fraud screening outside agent delegation does not itself require an S-classification.

8.3 Proposed S1-to-S2 integration
---------------------------------

Keep four distinct responsibilities:

1. Instnt assessment: identity/fraud-risk evidence, with its version, time and limits.

2. GAuth authority: a separate principal-rooted mandate evaluated for the actual action.

3. Platform permission: the destination’s separate credential/capability and qualifying authorisation decision.

4. Execution enforcement: apply the restrictive combination before the consequential action, preserving action binding and preventing bypass.

Instnt evidence can inform supplementary review or veto; it cannot grant missing authority. A probabilistic score, even wrapped in a deterministic threshold, is not by itself a qualifying deterministic CCPE evaluator. Two software services are not two authority artifacts. Identify the platform artifact’s issuer, lifecycle and action binding and validate the relevant profile’s ordering and controls before claiming S2.

No particular lettered CCPE profile is assigned here: identity onboarding, commerce execution and protected-data processing can involve different substrates.

A genuine, low-risk identity can lack authority. A validly authorised actor can still commit fraud. The combination addresses different risk dimensions without making either control redundant.

8.4 Existing safeguards, agent identity and GAuth: incremental underwriting value
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The limitation is not that existing controls have no insurance value.Content safeguards, fraud screening, least-privilege access and agent identity management can materially improve risk quality. Their underwriting contribution is narrower where the question is: what business authority did this particular agent have for this particular transaction, and was that authority enforced?

Content guardrails and S1 are not the same thing
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

“Guardrails” can mean content filters, prompt-injection detection, safety classifiers, deterministic transaction rules, human approvals or tool restrictions. They are not one homogeneous technology. Some are probabilistic; others enforce hard limits. Insurers should ask what each control actually prevents, on which routes and with what tested failure rate.

S1 describes a deployment that relies on platform-native identity and permissions without the separate GAuth mandate. A guardrail can be present in either S1 or S2. It is therefore misleading to label every guardrail solution S1, or to infer that an S1 deployment has no effective loss-prevention controls.

Entra Agent ID: valuable access controls, not automatically a business mandate
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Microsoft’s public documentation describes Entra Agent ID as an authentication and authorisation framework, including agent identities, restricted role assignments, delegated permissions acting on behalf of users, administrator-consented application permissions and inheritable permissions.[7] These are substantive preventive controls— not merely monitoring or an identity label.

Delegated API permission, however, is not automatically evidence of a separately signed business instruction with a particular amount, supplier, purpose, expiry and non-expanding delegation chain. The cited documentation does not by itself demonstrate that complete GAuth mandate-and-enforcement arrangement for the procurement example. Calling Entra Agent ID an “ID-bound mandate” should not imply that Microsoft already supplies the identical artifact or every control discussed here.

For an underwriter, Entra can help answer which agent is recognised, who it can act for, and which resources it can access. GAuth’s proposed additional contribution is which specific assignment the responsible principal has authorised, within which limits, and whether those limits were applied at execution. Preserve Entra’s permissions and safeguards; add and test the mandate layer rather than replacing identity management.

