10.1 Minimum test scenarios
---------------------------

The test report should record expected outcome, observed outcome, evidence and residual limitations for:

- a legitimate action within both mandate and platform scope;

- a genuine identity requesting an action outside its mandate;

- a low-risk fraud score accompanying absent authority;

- a child agent attempting to widen scope;

- generated instructions attempting to bypass a restriction;

- revocation during queued work and stale/replayed decisions;

- a relying party acknowledging a limitation then overriding it;

- concurrent/retried actions near an aggregate limit;

- loss or corruption of evidence and control-service outage.

Use synthetic identities/data where possible and avoid intentional harmful real-world transactions.

10.2 Concrete pilot: bounded procurement-agent purchases
--------------------------------------------------------

Proposed, illustrative and not yet agreed with an insurer. An enterprise uses an agent to buy approved maintenance components from participating merchants. Gimel supports the authority integration; the enterprise operates it; an interested insurer or reinsurer evaluates the evidence. A broker and counsel map the scenarios to candidate cover. An independent assessor verifies the agreed control boundary. Neither Munich Re nor Instnt is assumed to participate.

Scope and authority
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For this pilot, propose a maximum of EUR 1,000 per order, an EUR 5,000 shared daily purchasing budget across the participating agents, approved suppliers and product classes, and a 24-hour mandate validity window. These are illustrative test parameters, not product defaults, recommended insurance limits or bounds on consequential loss. The enterprise approves them before use.

The mandate identifies the principal, agent, permitted purchases, resources, validity and delegation constraints. A child agent may narrow but not increase these permissions. Independent platform credentials and permissions remain separate. Purchasing authority does not include changing payment destinations, expanding the supplier list or granting further authority beyond the approved delegation.

The integration covers request creation, merchant acceptance and order execution. An authenticated limitation notice and its verification result are bound to the exact order. Any merchant or alternate ordering route not covered by the integration is documented as outside scope; coverage of one route must not be presented as enterprise-wide protection.

The shared daily budget requires an atomic reservation/commit/release mechanism across agents, with an agreed day boundary and handling of retries, cancellations and partial fulfilment. GAuth mandate checks alone are not assumed to implement that accounting. If the mechanism cannot be demonstrated, the pilot must not claim an aggregate cap.

